  "id": "Compound::DB01124 - Gene::3758",
  "verbalization": "Tolbutamide, a sulfonylurea compound, is linked to Kcnj1 through a series of molecular interactions that involve antidiabetic agents and their binding to ion channels. The evidence suggests that Tolbutamide, as part of the sulfonylurea class, binds to Kcnj1 through a shared pathway with other sulfonylureas like Glimepiride and Glyburide. This binding is supported by the lowest common ancestor (LCA) relationship between Tolbutamide and these compounds, which all fall under the broader category of Sulfonylurea Antidiabetic Agents and N-Sulfonylureas. The mechanism appears to involve the direct interaction of sulfonylureas with Kcnj1, possibly through a common receptor or channel, which is further reinforced by the causal relationship between Tolbutamide and Porphyria Hepatic, which is caused by Glimepiride and subsequently binds to Kcnj1. This integrated pathway highlights a potential role for sulfonylureas in modulating ion channel activity, particularly through their interaction with Kcnj1, and underscores the importance of these compounds in antidiabetic therapy.",
